<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vlachs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vlachs is a historical name, used mainly by outsiders, for the Romance-speaking peoples of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and Aromanians. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> many lived as herders and soldiers across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a>, and they took part in founding the Second Bulgarian Empire and the principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Military Frontier the term was also applied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> settlers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vlachs.jpg" alt="Vlachs" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Théodore Valerio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Th%C3%A9odore_Valerio,_P%C3%A2tre_valaque_de_Zabalcz,_Romanian_shepherd_from_Z%C4%83bal%C8%9B,_1852.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
